Chapter: DtlsHello
  cites Foreword chapter Tls
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The ClientHello, ServerHello and HelloRetryRequest bodies for DTLS 1.3
 (RFC 8446 section 4.1, which RFC 9147 inherits whole). These are the
 message bodies the D2b framing carries and the D2a machine sequences.

 THE HELLORETRYREQUEST IS NOT A MESSAGE TYPE. This is the trap in this
 chapter, and it catches everyone once. DTLS 1.2 had a distinct
 HelloVerifyRequest; DTLS 1.3 does not. A HelloRetryRequest is a
 ServerHello -- same message type, same structure -- whose random field
 is a fixed magic value the working group published (RFC 8446 4.1.3). A
 receiver that switches on message type will never see an HRR; it must
 compare the random against the magic. Getting this wrong means the
 cookie exchange silently never happens, which means the DoS defence of
 D2a silently never engages.

 What is here: the bodies, the cookie extension, and the parsers that
 pull the peer's key share and cookie back out. What is NOT here, and is
 stated rather than implied: no certificate, no CertificateVerify, and
 therefore NO PEER AUTHENTICATION. A handshake built only from these
 bodies is an ANONYMOUS key agreement. It resists a passive eavesdropper
 and it does NOT resist an active man in the middle, who can simply run
 two handshakes and sit between them. Do not describe a connection built
 on this as secure.

 We say:

Section: Constants

 RFC 8446 section 4.1.3. A ServerHello carrying THIS random is a
 HelloRetryRequest. It is the SHA-256 of the string "HelloRetryRequest",
 chosen so it cannot collide with a real random by accident.

  dtls-hrr-random : List Integer =
    [207, 33, 173, 116, 229, 154, 97, 17, 190, 29, 140, 2, 30, 101, 184, 145,
     194, 162, 17, 22, 122, 187, 140, 94, 7, 158, 9, 226, 200, 168, 51, 156]

  dtls-ext-cookie-type : Integer = 44
  dtls-legacy-version : List Integer = [3, 3]
  dtls-supported-version : List Integer = [3, 4]

Section: Cookie Extension

 RFC 8446 section 4.2.2. The cookie is an opaque vector, so it carries
 its own two-byte length inside the extension's two-byte length.

  dtls-ext-cookie : List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-ext-cookie (cookie) =
    let inner = tls-be16 (list-length cookie) & cookie
    in tls-be16 dtls-ext-cookie-type & tls-be16 (list-length inner) & inner

 The server's supported_versions is a single version, not a list -- the
 client sends a vector, the server answers with the one it picked. The
 asymmetry is real and a shared encoder gets it wrong.

  dtls-ext-versions-server : List Integer =
    tls-be16 tls-ext-supported-versions & tls-be16 2 & dtls-supported-version

 The server's key_share is a bare KeyShareEntry -- again, no outer vector,
 unlike the client's list of them.

  dtls-ext-key-share-server : List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-ext-key-share-server (public-key) =
    let entry = tls-be16 tls-group-x25519 & tls-be16 32 & public-key
    in tls-be16 tls-ext-key-share & tls-be16 (list-length entry) & entry

Section: ClientHello

  dtls-client-hello-body : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-client-hello-body (random) (session-id) (public-key) (cookie) =
    let suites = tls-be16 2 & tls-be16 tls-cipher-aes-128-gcm-sha256
    in let exts = dtls-ch-extensions public-key cookie
    in dtls-legacy-version & random & [list-length session-id] & session-id & suites & [1, 0] & tls-be16 (list-length exts) & exts

 SIGNATURE_ALGORITHMS IS MANDATORY AND WAS ABSENT. RFC 8446 section 9.2
 requires it of any client offering certificate authentication, and a
 conforming server MUST abort without it. This body is shared by the TLS
 and DTLS clients, so both were sending a ClientHello no conforming
 server would accept -- and nothing could see it, because our own server
 does not inspect the extension and the only interop harness in the tree
 drives our SERVER with openssl's client. mosquitto's OpenSSL answers
 `missing sigalgs extension` and closes with no alert, which from the
 client's seat looks exactly like a server that never replied.

  dtls-ch-extensions : List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-ch-extensions (public-key) (cookie) =
    let base = tls-ext-versions & tls-ext-groups & tls-ext-sig-algs & tls-ext-key-share-client public-key
    in if list-length cookie == 0 then base else base & dtls-ext-cookie cookie

 THE SESSION ID MUST BE ECHOED. RFC 8446 section 4.1.3:
 legacy_session_id_echo is "the contents of the client's
 legacy_session_id field", and a client that finds anything else there
 aborts with illegal_parameter. TLS 1.3 has no sessions -- the field
 exists only so a TLS 1.2 middlebox sees a shape it recognises (appendix
 D.4), which is exactly why real clients put 32 random bytes in it and
 exactly why a server that ignores it fails against all of them.

 Two implementations that both send an empty session id agree with each
 other forever. This parser exists because openssl does not: its
 ClientHello carries 32 bytes, our ServerHello echoed none, and openssl
 answered INVALID_SESSION_ID.

 The field sits at a fixed offset in both TLS and DTLS: version(2) and
 random(32) precede it, so the length byte is at 34 whatever comes after.

  dtls-ch-session-id : List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-ch-session-id (ch-body) =
    if list-length ch-body < 35 then []
    else let n = list-at ch-body 34
    in if 35 + n > list-length ch-body then []
    else dtls-take-at ch-body 35 n

Section: ServerHello and HelloRetryRequest

  dtls-server-hello-body : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-server-hello-body (random) (session-id) (public-key) =
    let exts = dtls-ext-versions-server & dtls-ext-key-share-server public-key
    in dtls-legacy-version & random & [list-length session-id] & session-id & tls-be16 tls-cipher-aes-128-gcm-sha256 & [0] & tls-be16 (list-length exts) & exts

 The HelloRetryRequest: the same structure, the magic random, and a
 cookie instead of a key share. The server has allocated nothing at this
 point and must not -- the cookie is what lets it stay stateless.

  dtls-hrr-body : List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-hrr-body (session-id) (cookie) =
    let exts = dtls-ext-versions-server & dtls-ext-cookie cookie
    in dtls-legacy-version & dtls-hrr-random & [list-length session-id] & session-id & tls-be16 tls-cipher-aes-128-gcm-sha256 & [0] & tls-be16 (list-length exts) & exts

  dtls-is-hrr : List Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-is-hrr (sh-body) =
    if list-length sh-body < 34 then False
    else dtls-eq-at sh-body 2 dtls-hrr-random 0 32

  dtls-eq-at : List Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dtls-eq-at (a) (ao) (b) (bo) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then True
    else if list-at a (ao) /= list-at b (bo) then False
    else dtls-eq-at a (ao + 1) b (bo + 1) (n - 1)

Section: Parsing the Server's Answer

 Walk the extension list and pull out what we need. The extensions block
 starts after version(2) + random(32) + session_id + cipher_suite(2) +
 compression(1), and every hop is length-prefixed -- so every hop is a
 chance to be lied to. Each one is bounds-checked against the buffer we
 actually have, not against the length the peer claims.

  dtls-sh-ext-offset : List Integer -> Integer
  dtls-sh-ext-offset (b) =
    let sid-len = list-at b 34
    in 34 + 1 + sid-len + 2 + 1 + 2

  dtls-sh-find-ext : List Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-sh-find-ext (b) (want) =
    if list-length b < 40 then None
    else dtls-ext-scan b (dtls-sh-ext-offset b) want

  dtls-ext-scan : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-ext-scan (b) (off) (want) =
    if off + 4 > list-length b then None
    else dtls-ext-scan-at b off want

  dtls-ext-scan-at : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-ext-scan-at (b) (off) (want) =
    let etype = bit-or (bit-shl (list-at b off) 8) (list-at b (off + 1))
    in let elen = bit-or (bit-shl (list-at b (off + 2)) 8) (list-at b (off + 3))
    in if off + 4 + elen > list-length b then None
    else if etype == want then Just (dtls-take-at b (off + 4) elen)
    else dtls-ext-scan b (off + 4 + elen) want

 The server's key share: inside the extension, a KeyShareEntry of group,
 length, key.

  dtls-sh-key-share : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-sh-key-share (sh-body) =
    when dtls-sh-find-ext sh-body tls-ext-key-share
      is None -> None
      is Just (e) -> if list-length e < 36 then None else Just (dtls-take-at e 4 32)

 The cookie out of a HelloRetryRequest: inside the extension, an opaque
 vector with its own length.

  dtls-hrr-cookie : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-hrr-cookie (sh-body) =
    when dtls-sh-find-ext sh-body dtls-ext-cookie-type
      is None -> None
      is Just (e) -> if list-length e < 2 then None else dtls-cookie-inner e

  dtls-cookie-inner : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-cookie-inner (e) =
    let n = bit-or (bit-shl (list-at e 0) 8) (list-at e 1)
    in if 2 + n > list-length e then None else Just (dtls-take-at e 2 n)

Section: Parsing the Client's Hello

 A ClientHello is NOT a ServerHello with the fields in the same places.
 It carries a cipher_suites vector and a compression_methods vector that
 the server's hello does not, so the extension block sits at a different
 offset and the ServerHello parser above would read the wrong bytes.
 This is its own walk. Getting this wrong is what made the server find
 neither the cookie nor the client's key share on the second flight.

 Layout: version(2), random(32), session_id(1+n), cipher_suites(2+n),
 compression(1+n), extensions(2+n).

  dtls-ch-ext-offset : List Integer -> Integer
  dtls-ch-ext-offset (b) =
    let sid = list-at b 34
    in let o1 = 35 + sid
    in let cs = bit-or (bit-shl (list-at b o1) 8) (list-at b (o1 + 1))
    in let o2 = o1 + 2 + cs
    in let comp = list-at b o2
    in o2 + 1 + comp + 2

  dtls-ch-find-ext : List Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-ch-find-ext (b) (want) =
    if list-length b < 40 then None
    else dtls-ext-scan b (dtls-ch-ext-offset b) want

 The client's key_share is a LIST of KeyShareEntry, unlike the server's
 bare one -- so skip the 2-byte list length before the first entry's
 group(2) and length(2).

  dtls-ch-key-share : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-ch-key-share (ch-body) =
    when dtls-ch-find-ext ch-body tls-ext-key-share
      is None -> None
      is Just (e) -> if list-length e < 38 then None else Just (dtls-take-at e 6 32)

  dtls-ch-cookie : List Integer -> Maybe (List Integer)
  dtls-ch-cookie (ch-body) =
    when dtls-ch-find-ext ch-body dtls-ext-cookie-type
      is None -> None
      is Just (e) -> if list-length e < 2 then None else dtls-cookie-inner e

Section: Helpers

  dtls-take-at : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-take-at (bs) (off) (n) = dtls-take-loop bs off n 0 []

  dtls-take-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dtls-take-loop (bs) (off) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if off + i >= list-length bs then acc
    else dtls-take-loop bs off n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at bs (off + i)))
